course: everyday
lesson: 359
title: Promises to friends and to yourself
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Hannah in Auckland promised to walk home with her friend Lily every day after school. One day she wanted to get home fast for a TV show and left without her. Lily walked alone, got scared at the big crossing and arrived home in tears; her mum rang Hannah's mum. Hannah said sorry the next morning and waited for Lily at the gate every day after that — even when it was raining and the show was on.
explain: Josh promised himself he would save his pocket money for a new football. In the first week he spent it all on sweets and a toy he never played with. Nobody told him off — there was nobody to — but when his friends played with their new balls, he felt silly. He started saving again, and this time he kept the money in a jar where he could see it grow.
explain: Promises to friends let them feel safe and plan. Promises to yourself are how you reach the things you want. Both are broken in the same quiet way — by choosing what is easier right now.
explain: Tom promised his little sister his old bike, then sold it for money. Mum made him give his sister the money, and Tom said sorry and bought her a helmet as well.
explain: Mum once told Josh: «A broken promise to yourself has no punishment. That's what makes it hard.»
fact: People count on promises to plan and to feel safe.
fact: A promise to yourself, such as saving or practising, has no one to check it, so it needs extra will.
fact: Saving works better when you can see it grow and know what it is for.
fact: A promise to be careful with something borrowed includes paying for or fixing it if it breaks.
fact: Something you have promised to someone is no longer yours to sell or give away.
fact: Small lies again after a promise of honesty are common, but each truth told makes the next one easier.
fact: Being kind to someone new is a promise you keep every day, not once.
fact: Teachers can usually tell from a lesson whether a pupil has practised.
fact: When there is no outside punishment, the consequence is often missing out on what you wanted.
exercise: Why did Hannah leave without Lily? | She wanted to get home fast for a show | Lily told her to go on ahead | Hannah's mum called her home | She forgot Lily was at school
exercise: What happened because Hannah broke her promise? | Lily walked alone, got scared and cried | Lily got a lift home from a neighbour and was fine | Nothing happened to Lily at all | Lily went home with her teacher
exercise: Why do promises like walking home together matter so much? | People count on them to feel safe | They are only for fun, not for real | They are school rules, and you get a detention for breaking them | They help you get home from school faster
exercise: What put Hannah's broken promise right? | Saying sorry and waiting for Lily every day after | Recording the show for Lily | Telling Lily to walk faster | Saying the crossing is not scary
exercise: Josh spent his savings in the first week. Who punished him? | Nobody, but he missed out on the football | His teacher kept him in at break | His friends | The sweet shop
exercise: What helped Josh save the second time? | A jar where he could see the money grow | Spending it faster | Borrowing money from his friends whenever he ran short | Forgetting about the football
exercise: Lara promised to practise piano every day and did not. What happened? | Her teacher could tell and the lesson was hard | Her teacher could not tell at all | She played better than ever | The piano went out of tune
exercise: Ben promised to tell the truth and told a small lie again. What is true? | Slipping happens, and each truth makes the next easier | One slip means he can never be honest | Small lies don't count at all once you have made the promise | He should stop promising honesty
exercise: Chloe promised to be kind to the new boy, then laughed at him to show off. What put it right? | Saying sorry and playing with him | Saying she was only joking | Laughing at someone else instead | Avoiding him from then on
exercise: Max promised to help his friend with his project and did not. His friend did it alone. What would put it right? | Saying sorry and helping with the next one | Saying the project was easy anyway | Copying his friend's project and handing it in as well | Telling the teacher he helped
exercise: Isla promised Mum she'd be careful with her necklace and broke it. What was a fair way to make amends? | Saying sorry and saving to fix it | Hiding the necklace in a drawer so Mum would not see it | Saying it was already weak | Buying sweets for Mum
exercise: Tom promised his little sister his old bike, then sold it for money. Why is that wrong? | It was already promised to her | Old bikes cannot be sold to anyone | Little sisters cannot ride bikes | It is fine if you share the money
exercise: Mum made Tom give his sister the money. What else did he do to make up? | Said sorry and bought her a helmet | Promised her his next bike, then forgot | Told her the bike was rusty anyway | Kept half the money
exercise: Why are promises to yourself harder to keep than promises to others? | Nobody checks them or punishes you | They are always much bigger promises than the ones to others | Other people make them for you | They are not real promises
exercise: Josh's friend says «I spent my savings last month and it was fine». What is wiser? | Spending it all means missing what you were saving for | Saving is never worth it | It's fine to spend it all, because nobody ever checks | It's better to borrow
exercise: Hannah's friend says «you left her once, so what». What is true? | Once was enough to scare her; it matters | Once never counts | Lily should just get over it and stop being a baby | Lily should walk with someone else
test: «said i'd wait for Lily but my show's on, she'll be fine». What's the wise choice? | Wait for her as promised | Run home and text her from there | Tell her to run home fast | Leave her a note at the gate
test: Lily's mum on the phone: «She came home crying, she walked on her own». What's the best next step for Hannah? | Say sorry and keep the promise from now on | Say Lily is too slow | Say it was a one-off, so it doesn't really count | Stop being friends with Lily
test: «saved £3, spent £3 on sweets, then saved £3, spent £3». How much has he got for the football? | Nothing | £6 | £3 | £12
test: piano teacher: «Did you practise this week?» You didn't. What's honest? | «No, not much. I'll do better» | «Yes, every day» | «The piano was broken» | «My cat kept sitting on the keys»
test: broke Mum's necklace, she's not noticed yet, «she didn't notice last time, so it's fine». Is it? | No, tell her and help fix it | Yes, if she didn't notice last time, it's fine | Yes, necklaces break anyway | Only if it's cheap
test: you sold the bike you'd promised your sister. she's crying. what's fair? | Give her the money and say sorry | Keep the money, it was your bike after all | Say she can have your next bike instead | Say she's too little for a bike anyway
test: promised to be kind to the new boy, then everyone laughed at him and you joined in. Best move now? | Say sorry to him and play with him | Say you were only laughing at the joke | Join in the laughing again so you fit in | Avoid him so it's not awkward at school
test: «I promised I'd stop lying and I lied AGAIN, what's the point». Best answer? | Each truth makes the next easier; keep trying | There's no point, so give up promising anything | Lies don't matter | Promise something easier
test: mate: «you said you'd help with my volcano and you never came». You stayed home gaming. What's the fix? | Say sorry and help with the next project | Say volcanoes are boring | Say you were ill all weekend and couldn't come | Say his volcano looks fine
test: Why is a jar of savings you can see better than money in a pocket? | You can see it grow and remember what it's for | Jars make the money grow faster than a pocket does | Pockets lose money by magic | Shops don't take jar money
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a promise made to oneself is frequently more difficult to keep than a promise made to another. | No external party verifies or enforces it | It is invariably of greater magnitude than any promise made to another person | It is not a genuine commitment | It is enforced by law
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the sale of an article previously promised to another? | It is wrong, since the article was no longer the promiser's to dispose of | It is permissible provided that the price obtained for the article is a fair one | It is permissible if the promise was spoken | It is obligatory where money is needed
exam: It is given that a child, having promised to accompany a friend home, departed alone, and the friend was frightened. Select the response consistent with the established facts. | The promise contributed to the friend's sense of safety and its breach caused distress | The friend's distress was unrelated to the promise | The promise was without significance, since the friend knew the way home perfectly well | The friend bears responsibility for the breach
exam: State the consequence that most commonly follows the breaking of a promise to save money. | The person forgoes the item for which the money was saved | The person is fined by a bank | The person receives the item regardless, as savings are not required | No consequence follows
